  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1215 AM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nCurrent radar imagery shows a few strong thunderstorms lingering \nover Elbert and Lincoln counties as well as a couple of weak showers \nover the northern Colorado plains. Any storm that is able to \nstrengthen over the next hour or two will be able to produce large \nhail and torrential rainfall. However, we believe the severe \nthunderstorm threat to be nearing its end for the night as \nmesoanalysis shows an overworked atmosphere and decreasing CAPE. \nAreas of patchy fog will likely develop due to the low level \nmoisture left over from today's convection and small dewpoint \ndepressions. \n\nMonday afternoon looks to be fairly quiet as a weak ridge builds \nover Colorado and subsidence gives way to mostly sunny skies. \nTemperatures will remain slightly cooler than normal, with afternoon \nhighs mainly in the low 70s at low elevations, and 50s-60s for the \nmountains. Model guidance suggests a weak shortwave grazing \nsoutheast Colorado Monday night as well as an increase in wind \nspeeds at the 700mb level. Given the slightly above normal moisture \nalready in the environment, few sprinkles over the plains cannot be \nruled out late Monday night into Tuesday morning. \n\nThe upper level ridge tracks east and flow aloft turns southwesterly \non Tuesday. At least a few isolated showers seem possible over the \nhigh terrain due to the slight uptick in monsoonal moisture, with\nthe plains remaining dry. One point of uncertainty for Tuesday is\nthat model guidance suggests breezy southerly winds over eastern \nColorado/western Kansas which could lead to a richer atmosphere \nfor storm development, although instability should remain fairly \nlimited. \n\nA disturbance will track north from New Mexico onto Colorado on \nWednesday, which will bring a better chance for showers and \nstorms over the eastern Colorado plains in the late afternoon and \nevening. The mountains will see an increase in shower activity \nWednesday afternoon as well, but it remains unclear whether any \nshowers from the foothills would survive and make their way onto \nthe Denver metro or if low level moisture will be transported west\nenough. Moisture will generally remain above normal through the \nentire forecast period, and models have been consistent in \nbringing precipitable water values that are at least 150% of \nnormal to the region Wednesday through Friday. However, given the \ncooler temperatures expected to be in place, lifting mechanisms \nwill be needed for meaningful rainfall to come to fruition.",
